          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Abraham:</strong> So, let’s start by having a recap on some of the best methods for maintaining security to refresh your memory.</p>
<p>Businesses large and small can use different methods to maintain cyber security to keep their data safe online. These can give a basic level of security against the most common risks.</p>
<p>Intrusion detectors can be used to monitor a system and any unusual activity. Also raising awareness of all employees and their role in keeping a business cyber secure.</p>
<p>As we have mentioned previously, using strong passwords such as those using a combination of capital and lower-case letters, numbers, and symbols and multi factor authentication are important and having a password policy can help staff follow best security practice.</p>
<p>Controlling access to data by making sure that individuals only have access to data and services for which they are authorised. This may involve physical restrictions to certain premises or application controls limiting access to networks which we covered in the last unit.</p>
<p>Firewalls are effective gatekeepers and a major barrier to cyber threats, software and firmware updates should always be kept up with. Having suitable anti-malware software installed and regularly updated is essential to protect your system.</p>
<p>Ensure that data is consistently backed up using any protected back up devices, so that if a system is hacked or corrupted the information can be retrieved.</p>
<p>Remember make sure that your system is not vulnerable to the risk of cyber security attack as much as you can, by being aware of how cyber secure you are.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
        <section id="testing-tools"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Testing tools</h2>
              <p>One of the ways of keeping a system cyber secure is by means of vulnerability testing this involves a process of evaluating security risks in software systems to reduce the possibility of threats. A vulnerability can be with the hardware, operating system, the firmware or with a piece of software installed on a machine.</p>
<p>Vulnerability testing tools exist in a number of different models. Let’s look at some of the most common.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Scanning appliance
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>This is a device that sits within a network and scans all the other devices on that network reporting back any vulnerabilities that it finds.</p>
<p>The downside of this model is that it requires the devices it’s scanning to be online when it runs its scheduled scan, which may not always be the case if a machine is turned off.
Also, in a world where remote working is now more common, laptops may not always be connected to the same network.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u5_s1_p2_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Agent based
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>This requires the installation of an agent on the devices being scanned which collects information about the vulnerabilities it finds and sends these back to a central portal.</p>
<p>The benefit of this deployment model is that because it is installed, it can collect a lot more useful information.
The downside, however, is that the agent can increase the operational overhead on the CPU and the amount of data being sent back can increase the network traffic.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u5_s1_p2_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Security researcher
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Occasionally a vulnerability is discovered by a security researcher before the vendor of the software is aware of it. If that researcher chooses to publish the vulnerability to the public instead of the vendor before they’re aware and therefore able to patch it, this is referred to as a ‘zero-day’ because until the vendor is able to publish a patch for it, there is no known mitigation which is potentially very dangerous.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u5_s1_p2_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Penetration testing
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Penetration testing lives on a spectrum alongside vulnerability testing. Whilst vulnerability testing is an automated way of collecting details of the vulnerabilities, penetration testing takes it one step further with manual verification.
Penetration testing should be done on systems at least annually, or upon a significant change to the system. Because it requires human manual testing, it is more expensive than a technical vulnerability management solution. Therefore, it’s important to capture in the scope exactly which parts of a system are the most likely to be targeted and would have the highest impact to the business should they be hacked.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u5_s1_p2_slide4.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
